menu "FTM time engine"

config FTM_TIME_ENGINE
    bool "Measure gPTP time over FTM as a Wi-Fi coprocessor station"
    depends on HOSTED_AVB_CLOCK_LINK && HOSTED_AVB_BUILD_COPROCESSOR_HANDLER
    default n
    help
        For a Wi-Fi coprocessor that is a station (an endpoint's standby
        link). While associated, it asks the access point for a PTP
        compatible FTM burst every 125 ms, reads gPTP time from the
        Follow_Up the responder puts in each frame, and sends the host
        gPTP time against the clock link's timer. Needs
        CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_ENABLE_FTM_PTP (Espressif's PTP compatible FTM)
        and a responder that fills the Follow_Up. Enable
        HOSTED_AVB_WIFI_TIME on the host.

config FTM_TIME_ENGINE_KEEP_CADENCE
    bool "Keep to the session cadence after slow sessions"
    depends on FTM_TIME_ENGINE
    default y
    help
        Starts the next session no sooner than 125 ms on, however far
        behind the schedule slow sessions left it. Off, sessions that
        failed after about 1.4 s each left the schedule hours behind,
        and the engine then asked for FTM back to back to catch up:
        37 sessions a second for 25 minutes. Bench-tested 11-23: 8 a
        second straight after a 5 minute backlog.

config FTM_TIME_ENGINE_DROP_ACROSS_STEP
    bool "Drop a single measurement taken across a responder clock step"
    depends on FTM_TIME_ENGINE
    default y
    help
        A session with one usable measurement takes the responder's rate
        from the previous session. A rate more than 1000 ppm off means
        the responder's clock stepped in between; the measurement is
        then dropped and the session becomes the next one's anchor.
        Off, the measurement is used with the last good rate: one taken
        across a 250 us step went through 250 us wrong.

endmenu
